In a certain code:
'P ∆ Q' means 'P is the mother of Q';
'P ∇ Q' means 'P is the son of Q';
'P ◊ Q' means 'P is the husband of Q';
'P ⊥ Q' means 'P is the sister of Q';
'P ∥ Q' means 'P is the brother of Q'.

In a certain code, each word is written as three parts placed one after another:
(1) the number of letters in the word;
(2) the consonants of the word arranged in alphabetical order (a repeated consonant is written each time it occurs);
(3) the number of vowels (A, E, I, O, U) in the word.
For example, MARKET is written as 6KMRT2.
